1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,048
Five points, joined by springs. Some springs are stiff. Some are soft.

2
00:00:08,048 --> 00:00:12,484
We want two teams, and we want to keep friends together.

3
00:00:12,484 --> 00:00:14,380
Here is how.

4
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:22,618
The number on a spring is its stiffness. Five is stiff. One is soft.

5
00:00:22,618 --> 00:00:28,062
A, B and C are tied by springs of five, four and one.

6
00:00:28,062 --> 00:00:36,150
D and E are tied by a spring of five. Between the groups there is only a soft spring: two, and one.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,324
Two rules. First: every spring that crosses the line has to be cut, and the cost is its stiffness.

8
00:00:45,324 --> 00:00:51,152
So keep the cost small. Second: no team can be tiny.

9
00:00:51,152 --> 00:01:03,560
Why the second rule? Because if cost is all that matters, the cheapest move is to isolate one point. E alone costs six. That is a useless team.

10
00:01:04,410 --> 00:01:11,318
So here is the trick. Give every point a score. One number each.

11
00:01:11,318 --> 00:01:17,674
Stiff springs should keep their ends close. Soft springs can stretch.

12
00:01:17,674 --> 00:01:22,642
We do not pick the scores. The springs decide them.

13
00:01:23,492 --> 00:01:30,160
Now treat the scores as positions on a line. Let go, and the points settle.

14
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:37,236
Careful: if nothing pulls them apart, they all sit in the same spot. Useless.

15
00:01:37,236 --> 00:01:42,968
So we fix the length of the line. Like stretching a rubber band.

16
00:01:42,968 --> 00:01:52,736
Now look. A, B and C pull left; D and E pull right. The soft spring between them becomes a wide gap.

17
00:01:53,586 --> 00:02:02,078
So we cut in the biggest gap. A, B, C on one side. D and E on the other.

18
00:02:02,078 --> 00:02:05,866
The cost is three: the two weak hands.

19
00:02:05,866 --> 00:02:09,298
Both stiff springs stay inside a team.

20
00:02:10,148 --> 00:02:17,368
But wait. A computer cannot let go and see. It has to calculate.

21
00:02:17,368 --> 00:02:22,068
So it keeps a ledger: one row and one column per friend.

22
00:02:22,068 --> 00:02:30,128
Diagonal: the total stiffness at that point. Off-diagonal: minus the stiffness of that spring.

23
00:02:30,128 --> 00:02:35,360
Every row adds up to zero. That is how you check the ledger.

24
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:41,054
The ledger gives one number for any arrangement: the stretch.

25
00:02:41,054 --> 00:02:48,490
For each spring: its stiffness, times the squared gap between its ends. Add them up.

26
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:55,978
Stiff springs stretched far cost a lot. So the calmest arrangement keeps stiff springs short.

27
00:02:56,828 --> 00:03:03,856
Try a real split. Write it as plus one on one side, minus one on the other.

28
00:03:03,856 --> 00:03:11,196
A cut spring has gap two, so it costs four. An uncut spring costs zero.

29
00:03:11,196 --> 00:03:14,840
So the stretch is just four times the cut.

30
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:21,464
Good split: three, so twelve. Bad split: twelve, so forty-eight.

31
00:03:22,314 --> 00:03:26,894
So we want the calmest arrangement: the smallest stretch.

32
00:03:26,894 --> 00:03:36,610
Two catches. All-equal gives zero, but that is not a split. And with a free length, everything shrinks to zero.

33
00:03:36,610 --> 00:03:41,430
So: fix the length, ban the flat one, minimise.

34
00:03:41,430 --> 00:03:45,222
That answer is the second-smallest eigenvector.

35
00:03:46,072 --> 00:03:52,956
Now we have the scores. Sort them: E, D, C, B, A.

36
00:03:52,956 --> 00:04:00,008
There are four gaps. Try all four: six, three, five, six.

37
00:04:00,008 --> 00:04:04,016
Keep the cheapest. That is two.

38
00:04:04,866 --> 00:04:09,974
Notice: the gap nearest the middle costs five, not three.

39
00:04:09,974 --> 00:04:13,882
Counting points tells you nothing about stiffness.

40
00:04:13,882 --> 00:04:20,698
So we check every gap. Scores propose. The cut decides.

41
00:04:21,548 --> 00:04:24,088
Three things to watch.

42
00:04:24,088 --> 00:04:28,932
The scores can flip sign. Same teams.

43
00:04:28,932 --> 00:04:34,976
Disconnected graph? The whole method breaks, so check that first.

44
00:04:34,976 --> 00:04:38,644
And if two scores tie, sort stably.

45
00:04:38,644 --> 00:04:44,116
Now go try it yourself. Next module: Kernighan Lin.

